"The first verse of Surah at-Tawbah begins with the concept of 'bara'ah' (disavowal/immunity). In the dictionary, bara'ah means to be freed from something and to distance oneself from it. Here, however, as a political and legal term, it signifies the termination of a treaty and the end of non-aggression. The verse announces that this is an ultimatum from Allah and His Messenger to the polytheists with whom they had made a treaty.\n\nIn the second verse, the polytheists are granted permission to travel freely throughout the land for four months. This period is a respite for them to make their preparations. However, at the end of this period, they are reminded that they cannot frustrate Allah and that the disbelievers will be disgraced and humiliated. The view that the beginning of these four months was the tenth day of Dhu al-Hijjah is the predominant one.\n\nThe third verse describes the proclamation made on the day of the Greater Pilgrimage (Hajj al-Akbar). This proclamation announces that Allah and His Messenger are disassociated from the polytheists. The expression 'Hajj al-Akbar' has been interpreted differently; some say it is the Day of Arafah, while others say it is the first day of the Feast of Sacrifice (Eid al-Adha). What is essential is that this proclamation was an announcement made to all mankind.\n\nThe fourth verse states that the polytheists who remained faithful to their treaties are an exception. These are those who observed the terms and did not cause harm to the Muslims. It is commanded that they be granted respite until the end of their treaty's term. Allah loves those who are mindful of Him (al-muttaqin), and fulfilling one's covenant is part of taqwa (God-consciousness).\n\nThe fifth verse commands that after the sacred months have passed, the polytheists be killed wherever they are found, taken captive, and ambushed on their ways. However, if they repent, establish prayer, and pay zakah, their way is to be cleared. This offers the polytheists the choice of either death and captivity or entering Islam. Torture and mutilation (muthla) are forbidden in war.\n\nThe sixth verse commands that if one of the polytheists asks for asylum (aman), asylum must be granted to him and he must be given the opportunity to hear the word of Allah. This shows that even warfare has an aspect of mercy.\n\nIn the 6th verse of Surah at-Tawbah, Allah commands that if one of the polytheists asks for asylum seeking to listen to the word of Allah, asylum must be granted to him. Upon a polytheist coming to Ali and asking about this verse, although the interpretation was made that the verse only covers those who come for a religious need, the verse actually contains an unqualified expression of asking for asylum. In this case, the person granting asylum must do so taking into account that the other party will have the opportunity to listen to the word of Allah. Granting asylum to those who come for the purpose of listening is obligatory (wajib), whereas for those who come for worldly needs, depending on the situation, it may be permissible or not obligatory.\n\nOne of the conclusions drawn from this verse is that if a disbeliever wishes to learn the proofs of faith, it is obligatory (wajib) upon Muslims to explain these proofs to him. Furthermore, according to the scholars of jurisprudence (fiqh), if an enemy belligerent (harbi) enters the abode of Islam (Dar al-Islam) having received asylum for a legitimate legal purpose or for trade—even if he enters with a doubtful asylum granted by a child or an insane person—he must be protected. Envoys and ambassadors, on the other hand, inherently possess asylum. Even if the person who has been granted asylum does not believe after hearing the word of Allah, the safety of his life and property is ensured, and he is escorted safely to his homeland.\n\nIs this proclamation an injustice against the polytheists with whom treaties were made? Verses 7–10 explain this: The polytheists cannot have a covenant in the sight of Allah and His Messenger, except those who made a treaty near the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram) and remained faithful to their covenant. As long as they are true to you, be true to them. However, when the polytheists find an opportunity, they observe neither ties of kinship nor oaths; they try to please you with their tongues, but their hearts refuse. They sell the signs of Allah for a small worldly gain and hinder people from the path of Allah. Regarding a believer, they observe neither oath nor covenant; it is they who are the real transgressors.\n\nIn the 11th verse, if these polytheists repent, establish prayer, and give zakah, then they are your brothers in religion. The verses are explained in detail for a people who know. In the 12th verse, if they break their oaths after their treaty and revile your religion, then fight the leaders of disbelief. For their oaths mean nothing to them; perhaps then they will desist. That is to say, war must be waged against those who break their oaths and insult religion.\n\nIn verses 13–16, the believers are encouraged to fight against those who broke their oaths, plotted to expel the Prophet from his homeland, and initiated the first attack. Do not fear them; rather, fear Allah. The wisdoms behind fighting are as follows: Allah will punish them by your hands, disgrace them, grant you victory over them, bring relief to the hearts of the believers, and remove the rage from their hearts. Allah grants repentance to whom He wills. The believers must know that they will not be left to themselves without striving (jihad) as those who take no protector or confidant other than Allah, His Messenger, and the believers.\n\nIn these verses, the command to fight has five objectives: punishment, humiliating the enemy, helping the believers, bringing relief to the hearts of the oppressed believers, and dispelling malice and rage. Fighting must not be viewed merely as killing and destruction, but as an instrument of divine punishment; and the believers, while fulfilling this duty, must avoid unjust torment.\n\nThe objective of war is not merely to punish or belittle the enemy. The primary objective is to attain Allah's help and His good pleasure. This dispels the malice and rage in hearts and brings peace. In the verse, these five fundamental principles are listed: punishing the enemy, devastating them, bringing serenity to hearts, removing rage, and attaining divine help. All of these require that warfare be founded upon faith and sincerity (ikhlas). Furthermore, repentance, which is the ultimate goal of fighting, is emphasized: Allah grants repentance to whom He wills. This repentance can take the form of disbelievers turning to faith, as well as believers being purified from their sins. Allah granting repentance to whom He wills indicates that not everyone will benefit from this blessing. Conditions such as prosperity and hardship affect people's dispositions differently; some become arrogant in prosperity, while others take heed in hardship. This, too, depends on the will of Allah.\n\nIn the 16th verse, the following question is posed to the believers: 'Or did you think that you would be left alone?' Did you think that Allah would leave you without testing you, before He knows those among you who strive (jihad) and who take no intimate friend other than Allah, His Messenger, and the believers? Allah is fully aware of what you do; He will distinguish between those who strive and those who do not. The condition of the hypocrites and the sincere strivers (mujahidin) will be made manifest, and they will be requited accordingly.\n\nIn the 17th verse, it is declared that the polytheists cannot maintain the mosques of Allah. For while they bear witness against themselves to their own disbelief, their deeds have become worthless, and in the Fire they will abide eternally. The services rendered by the polytheists to the Kaaba are not considered genuine maintenance without faith. True maintenance consists of the physical upkeep of the building and the presence of a large congregation, while spiritual maintenance is possible through worship, remembrance (dhikr), and seeking knowledge within it solely for the sake of Allah. These services of the polytheists actually harm the spirit of the mosque.\n\nIn the 18th verse, it is stated that the mosques of Allah can only be maintained by those who possess four characteristics: belief in Allah and the Last Day, establishing prayer, giving zakah, and fearing none except Allah. These qualities must be combined so that mosques are rightly maintained and prosper. For the spiritual maintenance of mosques is only possible through sincere believers. Those who possess these characteristics are the rightly guided ones.\n\nIn verses 19–20, providing water to pilgrims and maintaining the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram) cannot be held equal to the deed of those who believe and strive (jihad) in the cause of Allah. These services of the polytheists cannot be compared with faith and striving; they are not equal in the sight of Allah. Those who believe, emigrate, and strive with their lives and their wealth hold the greatest rank in the sight of Allah. It is they who are the triumphant. This superiority is also emphasized in Surah an-Nisa. In the occasion of revelation (sabab al-nuzul), it is narrated that these verses were revealed when the polytheists asked the Jews, 'Are we more virtuous, or Muhammad and his companions?'\n\nVerses 21–22 report that these strivers (mujahideen) are given glad tidings of mercy and pleasure from their Lord, and of gardens (Paradise) containing everlasting bliss. That Paradise is eternal, and the greatest reward is with Allah.\n\nIn this section, it is emphasized that those who believe, emigrate, and strive with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allah possess the greatest rank in the sight of Allah. It is stated that they are the ones who attain salvation, and that this salvation is certain and absolute. Furthermore, the superiority of those who strive over those occupied with other acts of worship and service is supported by verses in Surah an-Nisa.\n\nRegarding the occasion of revelation of the verses, it is narrated that the polytheists boasted about deeds such as providing water (siqayah) to pilgrims and maintaining the Sacred Mosque, and asked the Jews whether they or the Prophet Muhammad and his companions were more virtuous. It is also related that the verses were revealed upon al-Abbas preferring to perform these services rather than emigrating, and that the Prophet (peace be upon him) told him to continue the duty of providing water.\n\nIn verses 23–24, the believers are commanded not to take their fathers and brothers as allies (awliya) if they choose disbelief. A warning is given that if worldly loves such as family, wealth, and commerce are held above the love of Allah, His Messenger, and striving in His cause, this constitutes defiance and transgression (fisq), and Allah will not guide such a rebellious people. These verses teach that believers must establish their priorities and, when necessary, be able to renounce even those they love most.\n\nIn verses 25–26, the Battle of Hunayn is described. Even though the Muslims were numerous in number, in order to show that they could not achieve success without Allah's help, they were first put to rout; then Allah sent down His tranquility (sakinah) and aided them with invisible armies, bringing them to victory. This event is an important lesson showing that victory comes solely from Allah and that relying on numerical superiority is wrong.\n\nIn verse 27, it is stated that following the Battle of Hunayn, many people repented and became Muslims. The Prophet's (peace be upon him) release of the captives and returning their property was a result of this repentance. While the verse emphasizes that Allah is Forgiving and Merciful, it also shows that even past animosities can be forgiven.\n\nIn verse 28, it is stated that the polytheists are impure and that they are henceforth forbidden from approaching the Sacred Mosque. Upon the Muslims' concern over a potential decline in pilgrimage and trade revenues, this prohibition is reinforced with Allah's promise to enrich them as He wills. The verse teaches that Muslims must not abandon religious rulings out of economic concerns.\n\nIn verse 28, Allah the Almighty declares that the polytheists are impure. This impurity is a spiritual impurity stemming from the belief of polytheism (shirk). For this reason, starting from the ninth year of the Hijrah, polytheists were forbidden from approaching the Sacred Mosque. There are differing opinions among the legal schools (madhhabs) regarding the scope of this ban: according to the Hanafis, the ban applies only to the Hajj; according to the Shafi'is, to the entire Sacred Precinct (Haram area); and according to the Malikis, to all mosques. The believers should not fear experiencing financial hardship because of this ban, for Allah will enrich them out of His bounty.\n\nIn verse 29, fighting is commanded against those among the People of the Book who do not believe in Allah or the Last Day, who do not consider unlawful what Allah and His Messenger have made unlawful, and who do not adopt the religion of truth. This fighting is to continue until they pay the poll tax (jizyah) willingly by their own hand while being subdued. The expression 'an yadin' means that the jizyah is paid out of hand, with submission, without delay, and directly by the hand of the payer. While jizyah is specific to the People of the Book, it is established by hadith that Zoroastrians (Majus) are also subject to the same ruling. Whether jizyah may be taken from the Arab polytheists, however, is a matter of dispute among the legal schools.\n\nIn verse 30, it is stated that the Jews said, 'Ezra (Uzayr) is the son of Allah,' while the Christians said, 'The Messiah is the son of Allah.' These words are baseless claims fabricated by their own mouths, imitating the blasphemous assertions of those who disbelieved before them. May Allah destroy those who utter such words; how deluded they are from the truth!\n\nIn verse 31, it is related that the People of the Book took their rabbis and monks as lords besides Allah. This means that they accepted that their clergy had the authority to legislate what is lawful and unlawful, and followed their words while abandoning the rulings of Allah. Affirming them when they declared lawful what Allah had made unlawful, or unlawful what Allah had made lawful, amounted to worshiping them. Indeed, when the Companion 'Adi ibn Hatim heard this verse, the Messenger of Allah explained it to him in this manner.\n\nIn verses 32–33, it is stated that the disbelievers wish to extinguish the light of Allah with their mouths, but Allah refuses except to perfect His light. Allah has sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth, that this religion may prevail over all religions, even though the polytheists dislike it. These verses give glad tidings that Islam will ultimately triumph and prevail over all other religions.\n\nThese narrations show that calling someone 'lord' is not a prerequisite for taking them as a lord. Obeying someone without regard to whether it contradicts Allah's command, and unquestioningly accepting their orders, means taking them as a lord and worshiping them. The Jews and Christians took their scholars and clergymen as lords by obeying them in matters that ran contrary to Allah's command. This constitutes taking lords 'besides Allah' and is polytheism (shirk). However, obedience that is in conformity with Allah's command is part of obedience to Allah and is legitimate.\n\nObedience to scholars is contingent upon the condition that knowledge is given its due right and that it is in goodness (ma'ruf). Giving knowledge its due right means following the truth and seeking the pleasure of Allah. Those who strive to make truth appear falsehood and falsehood appear truth are tyrants (taghut). Granting scholars the authority to legislate while disregarding Allah's commands is to take them as lords. The Jews and Christians attributed such lordship to their rabbis and monks, claiming that they were infallible and believing that they had the authority to alter religious rulings. It is this that the verse criticizes.\n\nAllah wills to perfect His light, even though the disbelievers detest it. They attempt to extinguish Allah's light with their mouths. Yet Allah will surely make His light shine brightly, exalt the religion of monotheism (tawhid), and make Islam triumphant. It is He who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth. This religion is Islam, which upholds the truth in all things. Allah will cause this religion to prevail over all other religions, making it manifest even though the polytheists dislike it. This is a fulfilled promise: Islam is supreme, and nothing surpasses it.\n\nO you who believe! Indeed, many of the Jewish rabbis and Christian monks unjustly consume the wealth of people and hinder from the way of Allah. Give tidings of a painful punishment to those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend them in the way of Allah. On that Day, these hoarded treasures will be heated in the fire of Hell, and their foreheads, sides, and backs will be branded with them; and it will be said, 'This is what you hoarded for yourselves, so taste what you used to hoard!' This is a warning to those who amass wealth and do not give its zakat.\n\nIndeed, the number of months with Allah is twelve. Four of them are sacred months. That is the correct religion. So do not wrong yourselves in these months. And fight against the polytheists collectively just as they fight against you collectively. Allah is with the righteous. 'Nasi'' (postponing a sacred month) is only an increase in disbelief. With it, the disbelievers deem a month lawful one year and unlawful another year, so that they conform to the number of months Allah has made sacred. Allah does not guide the disbelieving people.\n\nObserving Allah's sacred months is an important obligation for the believers. However, the polytheists do not recognize the sanctity of these months, and whenever they find an opportunity, they launch attacks even at Arafat. For this reason, in order to preserve the sanctity of the sacred months, which is the most sacred of all inviolabilities, one must not allow other sanctities to be violated. Otherwise, one would be wronging oneself. The verse in Surah al-Baqarah, \"Do not throw yourselves into destruction with your own hands\" (2/195), points to this situation. The purpose here is to prevent the polytheists from exploiting these months as they did in the Pre-Islamic Period of Ignorance (Jahiliyyah).\n\nJust as the polytheists fight against you collectively, fight against them collectively as well. This command is not bound by time or place; it states that fighting must be undertaken whenever necessary, regardless of which month it is. The concept of the sacred months eliminated the absolute prohibition of warfare that existed in the Jahiliyyah era. For unjust killing that is not done for the sake of Allah is always forbidden, whereas jihad in the way of Allah is an act of worship that must be performed in every season. The verses \"Wherever you encounter them\" (al-Baqarah 2/191) and \"Wherever you find them\" (at-Tawbah 9/5) emphasize this matter.\n\nThe prohibition of fighting during the sacred months remains valid unless one is subjected to an enemy attack. If the enemy launches the first attack, fighting for defensive purposes is permissible. This prohibition should not be understood as an instruction not to wrong yourselves by initially deeming warfare permissible. On the contrary, abandoning jihad during the sacred months and giving the enemy an advantage means exposing oneself to danger. Therefore, the verse indicates that jihad is necessary in order to protect the sanctity of the sacred months.\n\nThe practice of \"nasi'\" (postponement) mentioned in the verse is nothing but an increase in disbelief. The word nasi' literally means to postpone, to delay. The Arabs altered their calendar, which was based on lunar months, shifted the positions of the sacred months, and considered a month lawful at times and unlawful at others. This practice was intended to breach the prohibition of warfare and to adjust the pilgrimage season according to their own interests. By adding one month every two years, they would completely erase an entire year every twenty-five years.\n\nNasi' is not a custom unique to the Arabs; any kind of calendar manipulation carried out for similar purposes constitutes disbelief. Postponing or advancing times in order to make lawful what Allah has forbidden corrupts religious fundamentals. For this reason, the verse informs us that nasi' causes an increase in disbelief and leads the disbelievers into misguidance.\n\nThe polytheists try to conform to the number of months established by Allah by regarding the same month as lawful one year and unlawful another year. However, this is merely their arbitrary discretion. In reality, the sacred months are fixed as determined by Allah and cannot be altered. These manipulations are similar to how the Jews and Christians shifted their religious days by switching from a lunar calendar to a solar calendar. Any form of nasi' prevents acts of worship from being performed on time and violates religious sanctities.\n\nIn conclusion, the practice of nasi' is nothing other than going to extremes in disbelief. Showing reverence for Allah's sacred months is an obligation upon the believers. Warfare can be conducted at any time, but only for defense against enemy attack. Preserving the sanctity of the sacred months is possible by observing what is right and obeying Allah's commands. Allah is with the righteous and helps them.\n\nThe system of nasi' (postponing months) practiced during the Jahiliyyah era did not merely consist of delaying a single month. This practice led to shifting all of the sacred months and violating the sanctities established by Allah. The purpose was to violate religious rulings in order to secure worldly gains such as warfare or trade. This situation was characterized as a compounded form of disbelief, and it was emphasized that deeming Allah's prohibitions lawful is a grave deviance.\n\nVerses 38–41 of Surah at-Tawbah were revealed during the Expedition of Tabuk, which took place in the ninth year of the Hijrah. At that time, due to hardships such as intense heat, scarcity, and the ripening of fruits, some believers were sluggish in going out for jihad. Allah rebuked them, declaring that preferring the transient pleasures of the worldly life over the Hereafter is a grave error. A warning was issued that jihad is an obligation and that failing to comply with this command would lead to a painful punishment.\n\nIn verse 40, it is recalled that if the believers do not help the Prophet, Allah will certainly help him. The incident that took place in the Cave of Thawr during the Hijrah is cited as an example: Our Prophet (peace be upon him), with only Abu Bakr by his side, reassured him by saying, \"Do not grieve; indeed, Allah is with us,\" and Allah protected him by sending down tranquility upon him and supporting him with unseen hosts. The word of the disbelievers was debased, while the word of Allah remained supreme.\n\nIn verse 41, believers are commanded to mobilize \"whether light or heavily equipped.\" This indicates that every believer must participate in jihad according to their own circumstances. Jihad is carried out with both wealth and life; it is a sacrifice made against one's comfort and possessions. Recognizing that this sacrifice is good requires knowledge and insight. Every effort spent in the way of Allah is counted as participating in the battle itself.\n\nIn verses 42–45, the attitude of the hypocrites who did not participate in jihad is criticized. If the expedition had offered immediate gains and an easy journey, they would have joined. However, when faced with hardship, they lagged behind, and later they will swear oaths to make excuses. Allah knows that they are liars. True believers, on the other hand, never ask for permission to stay behind from jihad; their hearts are filled with faith in Allah and the Last Day.\n\nIn verses 46–47, it is stated that if the hypocrites had been sincere, they would have made preparations for battle. However, because Allah disliked their going forth, He held them back, and it was said, 'Remain with those who sit back.' Had they gone out with you, they would only have increased you in corruption and sown discord among you. Among you are those who would listen to them, and Allah is most knowing of the wrongdoers.\n\nIn this section, the profound meanings of Allah's address to our Prophet (peace be upon him), \"May Allah pardon you,\" are examined. Beyond being an ordinary tidings of pardon, this expression points to the Prophet's exalted responsibility and his role as an exemplar for the ummah. The Prophet is held accountable not for any sin, but even for a momentary lapse of mindfulness in the presence of Allah; this form of address emphasizes his human nature, thus protecting the believers from an excess similar to the Christians' extreme devotion to Jesus.\n\nThe verses indicate that believers should not ask for permission regarding jihad, because true faith requires not evading jihad, but participating in it whole-heartedly. In contrast, those asking for permission are in reality hypocrites who do not believe in Allah and the Hereafter, and whose hearts are filled with doubt. This behavior of theirs contradicts their claim of faith and reinforces the likelihood of their dishonesty.\n\nAlthough the Prophet's immediate granting of permission to those who asked for it may seem like an error contrary to divine wisdom, it was actually an irregularity stemming from his excessive tolerance and optimism. Allah did not want these hypocrites to take part in the battle, for their presence would have introduced corruption and discord into the army. Therefore, by holding them back in their places, He protected the Muslims.\n\nThe characteristic traits of the hypocrites are described: when good touches the Muslims, they are grieved, and when misfortune befalls them, they rejoice. They boast, claiming to have taken precautions according to their own lights. However, the Muslim knows that whatever befalls them is by Allah's decree, and relies solely on Him.\n\nTawakkul (reliance upon Allah) is not the abandonment of duty, but rather leaving the outcome to Him while carrying out Allah's commands. For the believer, there are two good outcomes: victory or martyrdom. For the hypocrites, however, a punishment is anticipated either from Allah or at the hands of the Muslims. Their spending (infaq) is not accepted because they do not believe; and their wealth and children are nothing other than a source of torment in this world.\n\nThe hypocrites' spending will not be accepted, whether they spend willingly or unwillingly. For they have disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger, perform the prayer lazily, and spend reluctantly. Because they do not believe in their hearts, these acts of worship feel burdensome to them, and they perform them purely for show. For this reason, their wealth and children are not a blessing for them, but a cause of torment in the worldly life; and they perish as disbelievers.\n\nThe hypocrites swear that they are of you, yet they are not of you; their hearts fail them out of fear. If they could find a place of refuge or a cave, they would flee thither headlong. They slander you concerning the distribution of charities (sadaqat); if they are given from it, they are pleased, and if they are not given, they become angry. Yet, had they been content with what Allah and His Messenger gave them and said, 'Allah is sufficient for us,' it would have been far better for them.\n\nCharities (zakah) are allocated to eight categories: the poor, the needy, those employed to collect zakah, those whose hearts are to be reconciled to Islam, slaves, debtors, those in the cause of Allah, and the stranded travelers. Allah has ordained this as an obligation. Sadaqah is a right given for the sake of Allah, carrying the condition of transferring ownership (tamlik), and given to the needy. Zakah is the right of the poor from the wealth of the rich.\n\nThose whose hearts are to be reconciled (al-mu'allafat al-qulub) refers to the share given in the early period of Islam to certain disbelievers or weak Muslims to strengthen the Muslims. However, Umar stated that this practice was discontinued once the Muslims grew strong, and Abu Bakr did not object to this. The Hanafi and Maliki schools of thought hold the view that this share has lapsed. According to the Shafi'i school, however, it may be reinstated in case of need.\n\nThe other categories are: slaves (al-riqab) to be emancipated; debtors (al-gharimin) unable to pay their debts; those in the cause of Allah (fi sabilillah), specifically those striving in jihad, as well as the poor undertaking the pilgrimage (hajj) and the pursuit of knowledge; and stranded travelers (ibn al-sabil). Scholars unanimously state that the most significant portion of 'in the cause of Allah' constitutes the mujahideen. Zakah can only be given to one of these eight categories; it cannot be spent on the wealthy or on other charitable causes.\n\nRegarding spending in the cause of Allah (fi sabilillah), it has been debated whether aid given to the mujahideen can count toward obligatory charities such as zakah. According to Elmalılı, the expression 'fi sabilillah' in the verse directly refers to those engaged in jihad; other types of charitable works are valid only in the case of supererogatory charities. Obligatory charities like zakah may only be spent on the equipment and supplies that the mujahideen require in jihad. These expenditures may also be made by delivering them not directly to the mujahideen in person, but to the commander; this is a matter of ijtihad. All eight categories of charities are based on poverty and need; the collectors ('amilun) and those whose hearts are to be reconciled receive it not as charity, but as wages or for the purpose of aiding Islam.\n\nIn the 61st verse, it is described that the hypocrites harmed the Prophet and said, 'He is an ear [listening to everything that is said].' According to the occasion of revelation (sabab al-nuzul), a hypocrite named Julas b. Suwayd said, 'We say whatever we wish, then deny it in his presence and swear an oath, and he believes us.' Elmalılı notes that the Arabs refer to a naive person as an 'ear.' This attitude of the Messenger of Allah is not naivety, but a mercy to the believers; he believes in Allah and trusts the words of the believers. The verse draws attention to the difference between faith and assent through the prepositions 'ba' and 'lam'. A painful punishment awaits those who distress the Prophet.\n\nIn verses 64-66, it is reported that the hypocrites feared the revelation of a surah that would expose what was in their hearts, yet they mocked and made excuses. They say, 'We were only joking'; whereas Allah responds, 'Was it at Allah, His verses, and His Messenger that you were mocking?' After claiming to believe, they openly displayed their disbelief; although some of them may be forgiven, those who persist will be subjected to punishment. These verses lay bare the evil intentions and insincerity of the hypocrites.\n\nIn verses 67-70, it is explained that the hypocrite men and women resemble one another; they enjoin what is wrong, forbid what is right, and withhold from spending in the cause of Allah. Because they forgot Allah, Allah also forgot them; they are the defiantly disobedient (fasiq). Hell has been promised to them eternally. The news of earlier peoples (the people of Noah, 'Ad, Thamud, the people of Abraham, and Midian) reached them; nor was it that they were wronged, but rather they wronged themselves.\n\nVerses 71-72 state that the believers are allies and protectors of one another; they enjoin what is good, forbid what is evil, establish prayer, and give zakah. Allah has promised them gardens beneath which rivers flow and pleasant dwellings in the Gardens of Eden ('Adn). The good pleasure of Allah (ridwan), however, is greater than all these blessings. According to a narration, when the people of Paradise attain Allah's good pleasure, they will realize that this blessing is the greatest of all.\n\nIn verses 73-80, the Prophet is commanded to strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and to be stern with them. Even though the hypocrites swear that they did not say anything bad, they uttered the word of disbelief and reverted to disbelief after embracing Islam. Despite having been enriched through Allah's bounty, they sought revenge. If they repent, it is better for them; if they do not, there is torment in this world and the Hereafter. Some of them made a covenant with Allah, but they broke their pledge, acted stingily, and lied; consequently, hypocrisy has settled in their hearts until the Day of Resurrection. Allah knows their secrets. Those who mocked the believers who gave voluntary contributions were made a laughingstock and subjected to a painful punishment. It will not benefit them if the Prophet asks forgiveness for them, because they have disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger.\n\nAllah knows the secrets and private whispers concealed by the hypocrites. He is the Knower of all the unseen. The hypocrites who mock those among the believers who give extra charity voluntarily and those who can find nothing to give beyond their bare efforts have, in truth, only made fools of themselves. A painful punishment awaits them. As for the Prophet, whether you ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness—even if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times, Allah will not forgive them. For they have disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger. Allah does not guide such a rebellious crowd of transgressors (fasiqun).\n\nJihad against the hypocrites is conducted through evidence and proofs; warfare is merely one form of it. Julas b. Suwayd spoke evil words regarding the Prophet (peace be upon him) and then swore an oath denying it, but Allah revealed a verse proving him a liar. Fifteen men from among the hypocrites set an ambush on the return from Tabuk to kill the Prophet (peace be upon him), but they fled upon Hudhayfah's warning. Allah knows their true inner state and thwarts their secret schemes.\n\nTha'labah b. Hatib asked the Prophet (peace be upon him) for wealth; the Prophet said that a little wealth was better for him, but when Tha'labah insisted, he prayed for him and his wealth multiplied. Afterwards, he abstained from giving zakah, which is why the Prophet said of him, 'Woe to him!' and refused to accept his charity. This incident shows the consequence of breaking a pledge and how hypocrisy takes root in the heart. Allah instills a hypocrisy that will endure until the Day of Resurrection in the hearts of those who break their promises.\n\nThe believers giving voluntary charity (nafilah) is praised. 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf brought half of his wealth as charity, while 'Asim b. 'Adiyy brought one of the two sa's of dates he had earned from a night's labor. The hypocrites accused them of showing off, but Allah disgraced them. Tatawwu' refers to non-obligatory acts of worship and is virtuous, provided one does not go to excess in giving charity.\n\nThe Prophet sought forgiveness seventy times for 'Abdullah b. Ubayy, yet Allah informed him that He would not forgive him. For he disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger. A crowd of transgressors cannot attain guidance. The hypocrites who remained behind from the battle were pleased with staying behind and said, 'Do not go forth to battle in this heat.' It is said to them that the fire of Hell is far hotter. Henceforth, no expedition will be undertaken with them, their funeral prayers will not be performed when they die, and no one will stand at their graves. Allah wills that they be punished in this world by means of their wealth and their children.\n\nFollowing the hypocrites of Medina, the condition of the desert Arabs (Bedouins) is recounted. Some of the Bedouins remained behind from battle under the pretext of excuses, while the outright liars simply stayed put. There is no sin upon the weak, the sick, and those who can find nothing to spend. However, those who requested permission to stay behind despite being wealthy are condemned. They will fabricate excuses to those returning from the expedition, but they will not be believed. The Bedouins are more severe in disbelief and hypocrisy; some of them regard spending in the way of Allah (infaq) as an onerous penalty, while others, believing in Allah and the Last Day, look for good. Allah admits the sincere into His mercy.\n\nThe foremost pioneers among the Emigrants and the Helpers (al-sabiqun al-awwalun) and those who followed them in good deeds have earned Allah's pleasure. Gardens beneath which rivers flow have been prepared for them. These are the ones who attain the supreme triumph. There are hypocrites among the surrounding Bedouins and the people of Medina; only Allah knows them. They will be punished twice, and then they will be driven into a tremendous punishment. The repentance of those who acknowledge their sins and mix righteous deeds with evil ones is accepted. Charity is taken from their wealth so that they are purified and cleansed, and that they may find tranquility through the prayers of the Prophet. Allah is the One Who accepts repentance and takes charity.\n\nThe believers are told that their deeds will be observed by Allah, His Messenger, and the believers. The forgiveness of some of those who did not participate in the expedition is deferred to Allah's decree. There are also those who erected a mosque to cause harm, foster disbelief, and create division among the Muslims. They swear, 'We intended nothing but good,' yet Allah bears witness that they are liars.\n\nIn this section, mention is made of the Mosque of Harm (al-Masjid al-Dirar), which the hypocrites built to inflict harm, bolster disbelief, and sow division among the believers. Allah commands His Prophet never to pray in this mosque. The mosque founded on piety from the very first day (the Quba Mosque or the Prophet's Mosque), on the other hand, is far more worthy to pray in. In that mosque are men who love to purify themselves from sins and strive to be thoroughly cleansed; and Allah loves those who purify themselves. The building of the hypocrites will remain a knot of hypocrisy in their hearts until their hearts are torn to pieces.\n\nAmong the Bedouin Arabs surrounding Medina and the people of Medina itself, there are also hypocrites. They have gone so far in hypocrisy and attained such skill in concealing their secrets and practicing dissimulation (taqiyyah) that without revelation, even the Prophet would not know them. Allah will punish them twice: once with the punishment of this world and once with the punishment of the grave, and thereafter they will be cast into the tremendous punishment of Hell.\n\nFurthermore, a group among the hypocrites admitted their sins, showed remorse, and mixed righteous deeds with bad ones. They punished themselves by tying themselves to the pillars of the mosque and begged Allah for forgiveness. Upon this verse, the Prophet was commanded to take charity from their wealth, thereby ensuring that they were purified and cleansed. Scholars have expressed different opinions as to whether this charity was the obligatory zakah or a specific charity taken as an expiation (kaffarah) for sins. However, the verse has been cited as evidence that zakah must be collected by the head of state.\n\nFor another group, judgment was deferred (murji'ah). These were Ka'b b. Malik, Murarah b. al-Rabi', and Hilal b. Umayyah, who had participated in the Battle of Badr and were delayed in repenting. The Prophet forbade speaking to them, and people fell into conflicting thoughts concerning them. Allah would either punish them or accept their repentance. This was a matter resting upon Allah's wisdom.\n\nAllah has purchased from the believers their lives and their wealth in exchange for Paradise. They fight in the way of Allah; they kill and are killed. This is a promise binding upon Him in the Torah, the Gospel, and the Quran. And who is more faithful to his covenant than Allah? Rejoice, then, in this bargain for the believers! The believers are those who repent, who worship, who praise Him, who fast, who bow down and prostrate, who enjoin what is good and forbid what is evil, and who keep the limits set by Allah. So give good tidings of the supreme triumph to them.\n\nAllah declares that He has purchased from the believers their lives and their wealth in order to grant them Paradise in return. Although this is expressed in the manner of a transaction contract, in reality, everything belongs to Allah. Allah encourages His servants to spend the life and wealth He has granted them for His sake alone, and promises eternal Paradise to those who do so. This is a tremendous grace and bounty from Allah. Complying with this contract, the believers sacrifice their lives and their wealth in the way of Allah; they kill and are killed. This promise is affirmed in the Torah, the Gospel, and the Quran. Allah does not break His promise; therefore, the believers should rejoice over this transaction and give glad tidings to one another.\n\nThe characteristics of these believers are enumerated: They are those who repent, those who worship, those who praise, those who journey (those who fast), those who bow, those who prostrate, those who enjoin good, those who forbid evil, and those who preserve the bounds of Allah. Fasting is described as journeying because fasting distances a person from carnal desires and takes them on a spiritual journey. Furthermore, fasting is a spiritual discipline and serves as a means for an individual to explore their inner world. The believers possessing these noble qualities makes them worthy of the divine promise.\n\nAllah has forbidden the Prophet and the believers from seeking forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they are relatives, once it has become certain that they are the companions of the Hellfire. Abraham's (Ibrahim) seeking forgiveness for his father was only because of a promise he had made to him. When it became clear that his father was an enemy of Allah, he immediately dissociated himself from him. This shows that supplication and seeking forgiveness for the enemies of Allah is not permissible. After Allah has guided a people, He does not leave them to go astray until He makes clear to them what they must avoid. For He knows all things. The dominion of the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah; there is no protector or helper besides Him.\n\nAllah granted repentance to the Prophet and to the Emigrants (Muhajirun) and the Helpers (Ansar) who followed him in his hour of hardship. This took place especially during the distressing days of the Expedition of Tabuk. Great hardships were endured during that expedition, and the shortage of riding mounts and provisions reached an extreme level. There were those whose hearts nearly swerved. However, Allah, through His favor, enabled them to repent and accepted their repentance. Indeed, He is most compassionate and merciful.\n\nFurthermore, Allah forgave the three individuals whose judgment had been deferred. These were Ka'b ibn Malik, Murarah ibn al-Rabi', and Hilal ibn Umayyah. They fell into such distress that the earth, vast as it is, became narrow for them, and their souls were constricted. They realized that there was no refuge from Allah except in turning to Him. Then Allah enabled them to repent and accepted their repentance. He is indeed the Accepter of Repentance, the Most Merciful.\n\nKa'b ibn Malik had not taken part in the Battle of Tabuk. Yet until that day, he had never stayed behind from any battle except Badr. During the Expedition of Tabuk, his financial situation was very favorable; he possessed two riding mounts. However, when preparations for the expedition began at a time when the fruits had ripened and the shade had become pleasant, he wished to prepare as well, but could not make up his mind. Constantly procrastinating by saying 'there is still time,' he was ultimately unprepared when the army moved out. Although he wanted to catch up with them later, he was unable to do so.\n\nWhen walking among those who remained behind in Medina, he was grieved to see no one other than hypocrites and those with valid excuses. The Prophet (peace be upon him) asked about him in Tabuk, and some made up excuses. Upon returning, after the Prophet stopped by the mosque and prayed, those who had not joined the expedition began to present their excuses. When it was Ka'b's turn, he decided to tell the truth and said, 'By Allah, I had no excuse whatsoever; I stayed behind at a time when I was at my most prosperous.' Thereupon, the Prophet said, 'This one has spoken the truth; stand up and wait until Allah reveals His judgment.'\n\nMuslims were forbidden from speaking with Ka'b and his two companions (Murarah ibn al-Rabi' and Hilal ibn Umayyah). People turned away from them, refusing even to return their greetings. Ka'b was resilient; he would go to the mosque and pray, but no one would speak to him. This isolation, which lasted for fifty days, weighed very heavily upon him. One day, he immediately burned an offer letter sent by the King of Ghassan. Later, by order of the Prophet, they were also separated from their wives. These distressing days lasted for exactly fifty nights.\n\nOn the morning of the fiftieth night, someone called out from Mount Sal', 'Glad tidings!' Ka'b immediately fell in prostration. Allah's acceptance of repentance had arrived. The Prophet (peace be upon him) had announced this glad tiding at the morning prayer. When Ka'b went to the mosque, the Prophet's face was shining like the moon. He said, 'Rejoice with the glad tiding of the best day you have had since you were born.' Ka'b wished to give some of his wealth as charity, but the Prophet told him to retain a portion of it. Ka'b then promised that he would speak only the truth from that point forward.\n\nIn the verses (119-123), the believers are commanded to fear Allah and be with the truthful. It is not permissible for the people of Medina and the desert Arabs around them to oppose the Prophet or fail to share in his hardships. Thirst, fatigue, hunger endured in the cause of Allah, and every strike taken against the enemy are rewarded. A reward is recorded for every expenditure, small or large, and for crossing every valley. It is not appropriate for all believers to go to war; a group from every tribe should deepen their understanding of religious sciences so that they may warn their people when they return from battle. One should first fight the disbelievers who are close at hand, so that they may find firmness in you. Allah is with the righteous.\n\nIn verses 124-127, the condition of the hypocrites is described. Whenever a surah is revealed, they mockingly ask, 'Which of you has this increased in faith?' The faith of the believers increases, and they rejoice. As for those in whose hearts is a disease, it adds disbelief to their disbelief, and they die as disbelievers. They see that they are tried once or twice every year, yet they do not repent nor do they take heed. Whenever a surah is revealed against them, they look at one another and then slip away. Allah has turned their hearts because they are a people who do not understand.\n\nIn this section, the issue of faith increasing is addressed. The hypocrites mocked whenever a surah was revealed, saying, 'Which of you has this surah increased in faith?' Yet the increase of faith is possible both qualitatively and quantitatively. Qualitative increase is the elevation of the strength and degree of faith, whereas quantitative increase occurs through the multiplication of the matters to be believed. Abraham's desire for his heart to be at peace is an example of qualitative increase. Moreover, as the number of things believed in increases, quantitative increase takes place.\n\nFor believers, every surah increases faith and brings them joy. As for the hypocrites in whose hearts is a disease, with every surah they add a new measure to their disbelief and hypocrisy. They are afflicted with trials once or twice every year, yet they still do not repent and do not come to their senses. When a surah is revealed, they look at one another and sneak away; Allah has turned their hearts because they are a people devoid of understanding.\n\nIn verse 128, it is stated that Allah has sent to the believers a messenger from among themselves. That prophet possesses dignity; any hardship befalling the believers grieves him heavily, and he is deeply concerned for them and full of mercy. Here, the attributes 'ra'uf' (kind) and 'rahim' (merciful) are given to the Prophet, which are among the beautiful names of Allah. These attributes emphasize the Prophet's compassion and mercy toward the believers.\n\nIn verse 129, it is commanded to say against those who turn away: 'Allah is sufficient for me; there is no deity except Him; in Him I have placed my trust, and He is the Lord of the Great Throne.' It is narrated that these two verses (128-129) were the very last verses of the Quran to be revealed. Furthermore, it is reported that during the compilation of the Mushaf, the written copy of these verses was found only with Khuzaymah ibn Thabit and was collected from him by Zayd ibn Thabit.\n\nFinally, it is stated that these verses at the end of this surah remind the believers of the Prophet's compassion and the importance of reliance on Allah (tawakkul). In addition, in a hadith narrated by Abu al-Darda, it is mentioned that whoever recites the prayer 'Hasbiyallah' (Allah is sufficient for me) seven times in the morning and evening will attain Allah's help. Surah at-Tawbah is thus concluded."